

Astrometric Simulation Engine (ASE)

Proton‑Laser Interaction & Cosmic Data Integration Framework

Author: C. Matthew Blackwell — Independent DesignerVersion: 1.2

Overview

The Astrometric Simulation Engine (ASE) is a multi‑scale computational architecture designed to unify astrophysical environmental datasets with high‑energy proton–laser interaction physics inside a secure, HPC‑accelerated simulation framework. ASE enables researchers to model plasma‑laser dynamics under astrophysically realistic conditions by integrating cosmic‑ray spectra, star‑formation models, interstellar medium (ISM) maps, and GEANT4 particle‑interaction datasets directly into microphysical solvers.

ASE combines a hybrid PIC/Vlasov–Maxwell engine, GPU acceleration, distributed HPC scheduling, and zero‑trust cryptographic security into a single cohesive platform.

Key Features

Cosmic Data IntegrationIngests HDF5 astrophysics archives, FITS telescope imagery, GEANT4 particle datasets, cosmic‑ray spectra, star‑formation models, and ISM density maps.

Hybrid Plasma‑Laser Simulation EngineIncludes PIC solver, Vlasov–Maxwell field evolution, Boris/Yaw particle pusher, Esirkepov/Yee interpolation, collision operators, and MHD coupling.

Proton Acceleration ModelingSupports TNSA and RPA mechanisms with configurable laser parameters (pulse duration, intensity, wavelength, focal geometry).

HPC‑Optimized ArchitectureMPI/OpenMP scheduling, CUDA/HIP GPU acceleration, InfiniBand interconnects, and Lustre/GPUDirect storage.

Zero‑Trust Security KernelAES‑256 data‑at‑rest encryption, TLS 1.3 data‑in‑transit protection, Merkle‑tree integrity verification, HSM integration, RBAC auditing.

Simulation Designer GUIQt/Python interface for configuration, 3D visualization, data slicing, and comparative analysis.

System Architecture

ASE is organized into five major subsystems:

Data Ingestion & PreprocessingStandardizes cosmic datasets and constructs boundary conditions for microphysical solvers.

Core Simulation EngineHybrid PIC + Vlasov–Maxwell + MHD architecture for multi‑scale plasma modeling.

HPC Infrastructure LayerDistributed compute scheduling, GPU acceleration, and high‑performance storage.

Security & Integrity SubsystemZero‑trust compliance with full encryption and integrity verification.

User Interface & WorkflowGUI‑driven simulation design, visualization, and cluster orchestration.

